ection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c07d6a46-4ba6-5448-9f13-79808c35273d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334639" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/6044d391016e37af929b36e89e0a802151b5710707cc510e9e7f70bf9a2d1c0d.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-2 \\\\ y=2t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/54bdfaf14b4f580e1476d4649e03cd71cb8203e23ae318daa65251fd7ce02ba1.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=-2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/edb5558ed7b5ee0e4aacb4eb67d74747fcc366f1508e16b3e99f3e0d5ee53306.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85735f54-9272-52e8-9f28-7a3c1a0339b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363525} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2808b0-31dc-5791-92de-e5c8e49aecb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/19f9668a913eb5d108bf1043d23ba277035f619eca48f6d428856a5ba77b981b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363526} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f6c5d7-e7da-50a7-b407-02bd702ace2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363527} {:answer/id #uuid "d23246aa-4c37-5a31-b95f-6ee253c36f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363528} {:answer/id #uuid "dd47c1c4-bb31-5345-b873-7e4ed23ef87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6f6c5d7-e7da-50a7-b407-02bd702ace2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363527} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa2f120-8b61-589c-a6d4-ca9e13198c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4dc3f93b-be82-5bba-ac8d-804adc4e23df" :question/math-academy-id "q-334641" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4t+3 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4t-3 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069892/assets/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)"}]}
